-- Hygiene (#481): an answering handler's state does not capture the body's variable of the same
-- name. `tally`'s state is named `n`, and so is the body's `let n = 100`; the state lives in a
-- cell the body borrows, and its clauses are functions of their own.
--
-- Answer: i32[100]: the body's `n`, after one note.
effect Log { fn note(x: i32) }
handler tally(mut n: i32): Log = { note(x) = { n = n + 1; () } }
main -> i32 =
  let n = 100;
  with tally(0) { note(1); n }
